// The wait group: a one-shot countdown that lets a process wait for N
// units of work. Pure Emo over the process primitives — a wait group
// is a process holding a count, `done` is one message, `wait` is
// another — so every target the package declares answers with the same
// observable behavior, and nothing here needed a compiler change.
//
//   const wg = sync.wait_group(3)  // three units of work ahead
//   do worker(wg)                  // ... each ends with sync.done(wg)
//   do worker(wg)
//   do worker(wg)
//   sync.wait(wg)                  // blocks until the third done
//   sync.stop(wg)                  // ends the counter process
//
// The count is set once at `wait_group` and only ever goes down: a
// wait group is single-use, and there is no `add`. A `done` past the
// count does not silently vanish — the call round-trips through the
// counter and raises, naming the caller's mistake. `wait` blocks until
// the count drains and answers immediately once it has; several
// processes may wait on one group, and each is woken. Every rejection
// raises an ordinary Emo exception saying exactly what failed.
//
// A wait group is a process, and a process that parked forever would
// hold the whole program open, so the lifecycle ends explicitly:
// `stop` halts the counter, and a `wait` still parked at that moment
// raises instead of hanging. A call raced by a `stop` — a `done` whose
// counter is already gone — drops like any send to a dead pid, so the
// discipline is the plain one: stop the group after your last wait.
//
// The counter owns the count and the waiting pids, and one process
// handles one message at a time — so the state needs no lock of any
// kind. That is the point: Emo has no shared memory for a mutex to
// guard (messages are snapshot copies), and coordination is what a
// counter process is for. Every message on the wire, in either
// direction, is a tuple tagged `"sync"` — the package name — so the
// group's traffic never collides with an application's own messages.

// Starts a countdown of `n` units and answers the group's pid — the
// handle every other call takes. The count must not be negative.
def wait_group(n Int64) Pid {
  if n < 0 {
    raise Exception.new(message: "sync.wait_group: the count must not be negative, got ${n}")
  }
  return do counter_start(n)
}

// The counter's entry: the waiter row is built here, inside the
// counter's own process, rather than passed across the spawn.
def counter_start(n Int64) Int64 {
  return counter(n, List.new([]))
}

// Announces one finished unit. Raises when the countdown has already
// drained: a done beyond the count is a bug in the caller, and the
// round trip is what makes it surface at the caller instead of
// vanishing into the counter's mailbox.
def done(wg Pid) Void {
  wg <- ("sync", "dec", self_pid())
  receive {
    ("sync", "underflow") -> {
      raise Exception.new(message: "sync.done: the countdown already drained")
    }
    ("sync", "ok") -> { }
  }
}

// Blocks until the countdown drains; answers immediately once it has.
// Any number of processes may wait, and each is woken. Raises when the
// group is stopped under the wait.
def wait(wg Pid) Void {
  wg <- ("sync", "wait", self_pid())
  receive {
    ("sync", "drained") -> { }
    ("sync", "stopped") -> {
      raise Exception.new(message: "sync.wait: the wait group was stopped under the wait")
    }
  }
}

// Ends the counter process. A wait still parked on the group wakes and
// raises at its caller. Fire-and-forget, so stopping twice is a
// harmless dropped send.
def stop(wg Pid) Void {
  wg <- ("sync", "stop")
}

// The counter process: the group's whole state is the count and the
// waiting pids, and this loop is the only code that touches them.
def counter(count Int64, waiters List) Int64 {
  receive {
    ("sync", "dec", from) -> {
      if count == 0 {
        from <- ("sync", "underflow")
        return counter(count, waiters)
      }
      from <- ("sync", "ok")
      const left = count - 1
      if left == 0 {
        release(waiters, "drained")
        return counter(0, waiters)
      }
      return counter(left, waiters)
    }
    ("sync", "wait", from) -> {
      if count == 0 {
        from <- ("sync", "drained")
        return counter(count, waiters)
      }
      waiters.push_back(from)
      return counter(count, waiters)
    }
    ("sync", "stop") -> {
      release(waiters, "stopped")
      return halt()
    }
  }
}

// Wakes every waiter with the given verdict — `"drained"` when the
// countdown finished, `"stopped"` when the group was torn down under
// them. The caller decides what the counter does next: keep serving a
// drained group, or halt a stopped one.
def release(waiters List, verdict String) Void {
  if waiters.length() > 0 {
    const waiter = waiters.pop_front()
    waiter <- ("sync", verdict)
    release(waiters, verdict)
  }
}
